import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Stdlib Migration to Handl — op Declarations, Axioms, and the Elimination of Per-Library Rust","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Stdlib Migration to Handl — `op` Declarations, Axioms, and the Elimination of Per-Library Rust","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-03T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Accepted. Builds on [ADR-0069 — Arrow-Native Modules](./0069-arrow-native-modules.md),\\n[ADR-0052 — Scheme IR Contracts](./0052-scheme-ir-contracts.md),\\n[ADR-0068 — Predicates as Types](./0068-predicates-as-types.md),\\nand [ADR-0051 — FFI Rust Binding Facade](./0051-ffi-rust-binding-facade.md)."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0077-stdlib-migration-to-handl.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0077-stdlib-migration-to-handl.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0077-stdlib-migration-to-handl.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0077-stdlib-migration-to-handl-—-op-declarations-axioms-and-the-elimination-of-per-library-rust" tabindex="-1">ADR-0077: Stdlib Migration to Handl — <code>op</code> Declarations, Axioms, and the Elimination of Per-Library Rust <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0077-stdlib-migration-to-handl-—-op-declarations-axioms-and-the-elimination-of-per-library-rust" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0077: Stdlib Migration to Handl — op Declarations, Axioms, and the Elimination of Per-Library Rust”">​</a></h1><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><ol><li>The stdlib is handl source. Every <code>plugin-lib-*</code> library is a <code>.hnl</code> file under <code>handl-packages/</code>; per-library Rust crates are deleted. Rust remains only for the kernel, the engines, the loader, the FFI dispatcher, the host and the core value/runtime crates.</li><li>Every public arrow carries a <code>---</code> doc comment (the API contract in prose) and an <code>axiom</code> declaration (input/output types, <code>guarantee</code>, optional <code>requires</code>/<code>termination</code>).</li><li>A pure-handl arrow needs no proof file: the contract cache infers each arrow once and importers reuse it (ADR-0069 §4). An <code>op</code> arrow&#39;s axiom is trusted, and its contract is enforced at the eval boundary.</li><li><code>op</code> declarations are the FFI surface — <code>op &quot;name&quot; : A -&gt; B guarantee: …</code> binds a native implementation; <code>cap</code> declarations state capabilities. Both are additive top-level forms.</li><li>Every migrated file declares its capabilities and follows the interface/impl split (<code>&lt;lib&gt;</code> port, <code>&lt;lib&gt;-&lt;target&gt;</code> adapter) for multi-target support.</li><li>The six-phase port plan, the per-file requirements and the per-library inventory are <code>project/rfcs/0077-stdlib-migration.md</code>. Each phase is independently shippable and reversible; pure-logic libs go first, native libs after <code>op</code> + axiom enforcement exist.</li><li>Native bodies are handl over direct C/POSIX FFI (ADR-0083) — no Rust closures. A native function is a leaf <code>Function</code> with no module scaffolding around it.</li><li>Each library&#39;s public API is preserved across its migration; a breaking change (the <code>Module</code>/<code>Plugin</code> → <code>Function</code> + <code>.hnl</code> switch) is gated behind the phase that performs it.</li></ol><h2 id="why" tabindex="-1">Why <a class="header-anchor" href="#why" aria-label="Permalink to “Why”">​</a></h2><p>The stdlib was mostly Rust scaffolding around handl logic because the eval-side module registry and the inference-side module loader were never connected (<code>HandlModule</code> closed the pure-logic gap). The correctness story — contracts, proofs, capabilities, documentation — lived in Rust, where it cannot be verified. Moving it into handl makes the stdlib&#39;s semantics checkable and deletes ~60 per-library crates.</p><h2 id="what-stays-rust" tabindex="-1">What stays Rust <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-rust" aria-label="Permalink to “What stays Rust”">​</a></h2><p>The kernel only: <code>plugin-lib-handl</code> (loader), <code>plugin-ffi</code> (dispatcher), the <code>plugin-engine-*</code> crates, the <code>plugin-lib-lang-*</code> self-hosting bridge, the host crates (<code>host-cli</code>, <code>host-lsp</code>, <code>host-tooling</code>), and the core crates (<code>handl-language</code>, <code>handl-runtime</code>, <code>handl-value</code>, <code>platform</code>, <code>plugin-module</code>, <code>plugin-loader-native</code>). Everything else — logic, declarations, contracts, proofs, capabilities, doc comments and native implementations — is <code>.hnl</code>.</p><h2 id="not-doing" tabindex="-1">Not doing <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-doing" aria-label="Permalink to “Not doing”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Keep the per-library Rust crates</strong> — that is the scaffolding this removes; their contracts stay unverifiable.</li><li><strong>One big-bang migration</strong> — phases are independently shippable and reversible; the stdlib is mixed during the migration by design.</li><li><strong>Prove the Rust bodies</strong> — the kernel cannot verify them; <code>op</code> axioms are trusted and checked at the boundary instead.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>~60 <code>plugin-lib-*</code> crates collapse into one loader + <code>.hnl</code> files; each library&#39;s logic, types, contracts, capabilities and docs live in one file.</li><li>Trusted <code>op</code> axioms: a native body that violates its declared <code>guarantee</code> is caught by boundary checks or tests, not by the proof system.</li><li>Runtime <code>requires</code> checking adds overhead at foreign calls; a production mode may disable it once inference and testing carry the weight.</li><li><code>op</code>/<code>cap</code> are two new top-level forms; the lexer and Pratt parser must support them. Native crates that used <code>Module</code>/<code>Plugin</code> switch to <code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code>/<code>HandlModule</code> — a breaking change gated behind Phase 4.</li></ul><h2 id="enforcement" tabindex="-1">Enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to “Enforcement”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>handl-packages/*/index.hnl</code> — the doc comment + <code>axiom</code> on every public arrow.</li><li><code>crates/plugin-lib-stdlib/build.rs</code>, <code>crates/plugin-lib-stdlib/src/packages.rs</code> — vendoring and the package table (ADR-0160).</li><li><code>crates/plugin-ffi</code> — the <code>op</code> boundary where <code>requires</code> is checked.</li></ul><h2 id="revisit-if" tabindex="-1">Revisit if <a class="header-anchor" href="#revisit-if" aria-label="Permalink to “Revisit if”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A native library cannot express its contract as an <code>op</code> axiom + handl interface, or boundary checking costs more than the correctness it buys.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to “Related”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0069-arrow-native-modules.html">ADR-0069 — Arrow-Native Modules</a> — the module system this migration completes (Phase 7).</li><li><a href="./0052-scheme-ir-contracts.html">ADR-0052 — Scheme IR Contracts</a> — the <code>ArrSource::Foreign</code> / <code>Scheme</code> IR this builds on.</li><li><a href="./0068-predicates-as-types.html">ADR-0068 — Predicates as Types</a> — the refinement types axioms use.</li><li><a href="./0051-ffi-rust-binding-facade.html">ADR-0051 — FFI Rust Binding Façade</a> — the prior Rust-side declaration model this replaces.</li><li><a href="./0070-refinement-inference-scope.html">ADR-0070 — Refinement Inference Scope</a> — the inference engine behind the contract cache.</li></ul>`,17)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};